ass=3D"spoiler-btn contenteditable">=D0=9F=D0=BE=D0=BA=D0=B0=D0=B7=D0=B0=D1=
=82=D1=8C / =D0=A1=D0=BA=D1=80=D1=8B=D1=82=D1=8C =D1=82=D0=B5=D0=BA=D1=81=
=D1=82</a></div>
<div id=3D"sp55c53f64e6cd8ef54e" class=3D"text_spoiler" style=3D"display: n=
one;">
<div class=3D"contenteditable">In EFI mode, the default Microsoft standard =
EFI boot loader is used.<br><br>To expand functionality when booting in UEF=
I mode, you can switch to the GRUB2 bootloader.<br>However, booting with Se=
cure Boot enabled on all computers is not guaranteed. So it's better to tur=
n it off<br>When booting from GRUB2, there are memory checking utilities an=
d you can also add various<br>Linux images.<br><br>Switch between EFI loade=
rs Microsoft or GRUB2, you need to run the batch file on a flash drive in t=
he EFI/boot folder<br>_MICROSOFT_EFI.bat - switches to the Microsoft bootlo=
ader<br>_GRUB2_EFI.bat - switches to GRUB2 bootloader<br><br>You can always=
 see which boot loader is active by file in the EFI/boot folder<br>[MICROSO=
FT_MODE] - Microsoft loaders are active<br>[GRUB2_MODE] - GRUB2 boot loader=
s are active</div>
</div>
</div>
<p><br><b><span style=3D"color:#2969b0;">I do not have a flash drive, how d=
o I quickly boot from my computer hard drive?:</span></b></p>
<div class=3D"dle-spoiler noncontenteditable">
<div class=3D"title_spoiler"><a href=3D"https://sergeistrelec.name/descript=
ion1_x64_winpe_en.html#" data-id=3D"sp0088293e85cbb3f636" class=3D"spoiler-=
btn-svg"><svg width=3D"18" height=3D"18" fill=3D"currentColor" viewBox=3D"0=
 0 20 20"><path id=3D"svg-sp0088293e85cbb3f636" d=3D"M17.418 6.109c0.272-0.=
268 0.709-0.268 0.979 0s0.271 0.701 0 0.969l-7.908 7.83c-0.27 0.268-0.707 0=
.268-0.979 0l-7.908-7.83c-0.27-0.268-0.27-0.701 0-0.969s0.709-0.268 0.979 0=
l7.419 7.141 7.418-7.141z"></path></svg></a><a href=3D"https://sergeistrele=
c.name/description1_x64_winpe_en.html#" data-id=3D"sp0088293e85cbb3f636" cl=
ass=3D"spoiler-btn contenteditable">Show / Hide text</a></div>
<div id=3D"sp0088293e85cbb3f636" class=3D"text_spoiler" style=3D"display: n=
one;">
<div class=3D"contenteditable">
<p>No problem! To boot quickly from your computer hard drive:</p>
<p><br></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#169179;"><b>Option 1:</b></span><br>Download WinPE=
 ISO Image</p>
<p>Extract the SSTR folder from the ISO image to the root of any disk / par=
tition, it is better, of course, not the system one.<br>Run the file in the=
 SSTR folder</p>
<p><br></p>
<p>Boot_x64_WinPE.cmd - for loading WinPE x64 (run as Administrator)</p>
<p><br></p>
<p>The computer will automatically reboot to WinPE.<br>After rebooting from=
 WinPE, the computer will boot normally</p>
<p><br></p>
<p>After normal PC boot, remove WinPE boot entry from BCD by running the fi=
le<br>BCD_recovery.cmd (run as Administrator)<br>or manually uninstall with=
 BOOTICE</p>
<p><br></p>
<p><b>Possible applications:</b><br>For example, remote support.<br>The cli=
ent only needs to download the image, extract the SSTR folder and start the=
 batch file.<br>And you already have to remotely connect to the PC.</p>
<p><br></p>
<p>For beginners: if you are reinstalling Windows, you do not need to do an=
ything with the BCD.<br>It's already new there.</p>
<p><br></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#169179;"><b>Option 2:</b></span><br>Add WinPE to t=
he Windows boot menu. This way you can use WinPE on an ongoing basis.</p>
<p><br></p>
<p>Download WinPE ISO image<br>Extract the SSTR folder from the ISO image t=
o the root of any disk/partition, preferably not the system one, of course.=
<br>You can create a small separate partition for these purposes, and even =
make it hidden after adding it to the boot menu.<br>In the SSTR folder ther=
e is a folder called Add_BOOT_menu_Windows.<br>Follow the instructions in t=
he Readme.txt file.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
